Llmpalooza's split of the ExLlamaV3 kernels its native linear launches from
upstream's ATen host wrappers (D-053, D-066, D-080), for three files that
hold both: quant/reconstruct.cu (the reconstruction kernels, plain and
fused with both Hadamard transforms), quant/hadamard.cu (the 128-point
Hadamard kernels) and add.cu (the bias add, add_kernel_hhh). The kernels
are unchanged; what is removed is host code:

- the ATen, c10 and PyTorch-API includes (reconstruct.cuh, hadamard's
  quantize.cuh, add.cuh, and bits_k.cuh, which includes c10), which only
  the host wrappers used;
- the host wrappers themselves (reconstruct_slice, reconstruct_had_slice,
  reconstruct, the batched variants, had_r_128 and its dual and batched
  variants, add_gr, add, copy2d_gr and the MoE bias adds), which use
  ATen tensors, PyTorch's current stream and TORCH_CHECK and end the
  process on a CUDA error (cuda_check). Llmpalooza's launchers in
  src/kernels/exl3/ replace them and return errors as values;
- reconstruct.cu's constexpr instance tables, which instantiate every
  rate, codebook and half-integer rate. Llmpalooza instantiates only the
  kernels it launches (llmp/llmp_exl3_kernels.cu, patch 0002).

It also drops the `register` storage class from two local arrays in
reconstruct.cu, as 0001 does in exl3_gemm_inner.cuh: it has no effect, is
ill-formed since C++17, and NVCC's front end rejects it with a Clang host
compiler. The kernels' SASS equals the reference build's (docs/licensing.md).

